;; tags: control-flow, loops, while
;; expect: 1
;; A `while` loop is the structural inverse of `dotimes`: the test runs
;; *before* the body, so codegen emits `b <test>` to skip into the test
;; first, then label-body, body, label-test, evaluate predicate, b.ne
;; label-body. Two branches per iteration -- one forward (initial
;; skip), one backward (loop edge). We repeatedly halve `n` with
;; integer division until it hits 1, starting from 1000:
;; 1000->500->250->125->62->31->15->7->3->1 (9 iterations).
(defun main ()
  (let ((n 1000))
    (while (> n 1)
      (set! n (/ n 2)))
    (format #t "~D~%" n)))
